@node Low Module
@chapter Low Module

The protocols in the low module provide the basic ``storage'' aspect of
a package system without additional logic or consideration of the
package system consistency rules:

@itemize

@item
Predicates for symbol and package objects and construction of symbol and
package objects

@item
Accessors for ``slots'' of symbols and packages

@item
Registration of package objects in the environment and retrieval of
package objects from the environment

@end itemize

The functions in this module accept only strings where names are
required and only symbol objects and package objects (not designators)
where symbols and packages are required.

@menu
* Low Symbol Functions::
* Low Package Functions::
* Low Package-symbol Relation Functions::
* Low Environment Functions::
@end menu

@node Low Symbol Functions
@section Low Symbol Functions

This protocol consists mostly of accessor-like generic functions that
operator on symbol objects.  Some of the functions are called directly
by functions in the @ref{High Module} while others are called via
functions of the @ref{Middle Module}.

@defgena{symbolp,@lowpackage{}} client object

Return true if @var{object} is a symbol object @emph{in the package
system specified by @var{client}}.

@lowimplements{symbolp,f,symbolp}

The default method returns @code{nil} for all objects so that clients
can simply define a method that is specialized to the respective client
class and the associated symbol class and returns @emph{true}.
@end deffn

@defgena{symbol-name,@lowpackage{}} client symbol

Return the string that is the name of @var{symbol} @emph{in the package
system specified by @var{client}}.

@lowimplements{symbol-name,f,symbol-name}
@end deffn

@defgena{symbol-package,@lowpackage{}} client symbol

Return the package object that is the home package of @var{symbol} or
@code{nil} if @var{symbol} is uninterned @emph{in the package system
specified by @var{client}}.

@lowimplements{symbol-package,f,symbol-package}
@end deffn

@defgena{(setf symbol-package),@lowpackage{}} new-value client symbol

Set the home package of @var{symbol} to @var{new-value} @emph{in the
package system specified by @var{client}} and return @var{new-value}.

@var{new-value} is either @code{nil} or a package object.
@end deffn

@defgena{make-symbol,@lowpackage{}} client name package

Create and return a symbol object with name @var{name} and home package
@var{package} @emph{in the package system specified by @var{client}}.

@var{name} is a string and @var{package} is either @code{nil} or a
package object.  If @var{package} is @code{nil}, an uninterned symbol is
created and returned.

@lowimplements{make-symbol,f,make-symbol}
@end deffn

@node Low Package Functions
@section Low Package Functions

This protocol consists of accessor-like generic functions that operate
on package objects.  Some of the functions are called directly by
functions in the @ref{High Module} while others are called via functions
in the @ref{Middle Module}.

@defgena{packagep,@lowpackage{}} client object

Return @emph{true} if @var{object} is a package object @emph{in the
package system specified by @var{client}}.

@lowimplements{packagep,f,packagep}

The default method returns @code{nil} for all objects so that clients
can simply define a method that is specialized to the respective client
class and the associated package class and returns @emph{true}.
@end deffn

@defgena{name,@lowpackage{}} client package

Return @code{nil} or a string that is the name of the package object
@var{package} @emph{in the package system specified by @var{client}}.

The higher layers of @sysname{} use the name @code{nil} to indicate that
@var{package} has been deleted.

@lowimplements{package-name,f,package-name}
@end deffn

@defgena{(setf name),@lowpackage{}} new-value client package

Set the name of the package object @var{package} to @var{new-value}
@emph{in the package system specified by @var{client}} and return
@var{new-value}.

@var{new-value} is either @code{nil} or a string.  The higher layers of
@sysname{} use the name @code{nil} to indicate that @var{package} has
been deleted.

In accordance with the role of the low module, this function does not
check whether @var{new-name} is the name or the nickname of an existing
package nor does it register @var{package} in the environment under the
provided name.  This function is called by
@genref{rename-package,@middlepackage{}} and
@genref{delete-package,@middlepackage{}}.
@end deffn

@defgena{nicknames,@lowpackage{}} client package

Return the list of nicknames of @var{package} @emph{in the package
system specified by @var{client}}.

@var{package} is a package object.  The returned list is not freshly
allocated.

@lowimplements{package-nicknames,f,package-nicknames}
@end deffn

@defgena{(setf nicknames),@lowpackage{}} new-value client package

Set the nicknames of @var{package} to @var{new-value} @emph{in the
package system specified by @var{client}} and return @var{new-value}.

@var{new-value} is a list of strings.  This function does not copy the
@var{new-value} list, so callers should make sure this list is not used
after being passed as an argument to this function.  @var{package} is a
package object.

This function only sets the nicknames ``slot'' of @var{package}.  This
function does not check whether @var{new-value} contains a string that
is already the name or the nickname of any existing package nor does it
register @var{package} in the environment under the provided names.
This function is called by @genref{make-package,@middlepackage{}} and
@genref{rename-package,@middlepackage{}}.
@end deffn

@defgena{local-nicknames,@lowpackage{}} client package

@cindex package-local nicknames
@partofpln

Return an @spectermref{alist} which describes the
@termrefl{package-local nickname,local nicknames} which are defined in
@var{package} @emph{in the package system specified by @var{client}}.

@var{package} is a package object.

Elements of the returned alist are of the form
@code{(@var{local-nickname} . @var{nicknamed-package})} where
@var{local-nickname} is a string and @var{nicknamed-package} is a
package object.  The returned list is not freshly allocated.

This function can be used to implement and customize the semi-standard
function @funref{package-local-nicknames,@toppackage{}}.
@end deffn

@defgena{(setf local-nicknames),@lowpackage{}} new-value client package

@cindex package-local nicknames
@partofpln

Set the @termrefl{package-local nickname,local nicknames} of
@var{package} to @var{new-value} @emph{in the package system specified
by @var{client}} and return @var{new-value}.

@var{new-value} is an @spectermref{alist} with elements of the form
@code{(@var{local-nickname} . @var{nicknamed-package})} where
@var{local-nickname} is a string and @var{nicknamed-package} is a
package object.  This function does not copy the @var{new-value} list,
so callers should make sure this list is not used after being passed as
an argument to this function.  @var{package} is a package object.

This function only sets the @t{local-nicknames} ``slot'' in
@var{package}.  It does not update the @t{locally-nicknamed-by}
``slots'' of the packages in @var{new-value} nor does it check the new
nicknames in any way.  This function is called by
@genref{add-local-nickname,@middlepackage{}} and
@genref{remove-local-nickname,@middlepackage{}}.
@end deffn

@defgena{locally-nicknamed-by,@lowpackage{}} client package

@cindex package-local nicknames
@partofpln

Return a list of package objects that refer to @var{package} via one or
more @termrefl{package-local nickname,local nicknames} @emph{in the
package system specified by @var{client}}.

@var{package} is a package object. The returned list is not freshly
allocated.

This function can be used to implement and customize the semi-standard
function @funref{package-locally-nicknamed-by-list,@toppackage{}}.
@end deffn

@defgena{(setf locally-nicknamed-by),@lowpackage{}} new-value client package

@cindex package-local nicknames
@partofpln

Set the list of package objects that refer to @var{package} via one or
more @termrefl{package-local nickname,local nicknames} to
@var{new-value} @emph{in the package system specified by @var{client}}
and return @var{new-value}.

@var{new-value} is a list of package objects without duplicates.  This
function does not copy the @var{new-value} list, so callers should make
sure this list is not used after being passed as an argument to this
function.  @var{package} is a package object.

This function only sets the @t{locally-nicknamed-by} ``slot'' in
@var{package}.  It does not update the packages in @var{new-value} in
any way nor does it check the list of packages in any way.  This
function is called by @genref{add-local-nickname,@middlepackage{}} and
@genref{remove-local-nickname,@middlepackage{}}.
@end deffn

@defgena{use-list,@lowpackage{}} client package

Return a list of package objects that contains the packages which
@var{package} uses @emph{in the package system specified by
@var{client}}.

@var{package} is a package object.  The returned list is not freshly
allocated.

@lowimplements{package-use-list,f,package-use-list}
@end deffn

@defgena{(setf use-list),@lowpackage{}} new-value client package

Set the list of used packages of @var{package} to the list of package
objects @var{new-value} @emph{in the package system specified by
@var{client}} and return @var{new-value}.

@var{package} must be a package object.  @var{new-value} is a list of
package objects that become the new list of used packages for
@var{package}.  This function does not copy the @var{new-value} list, so
callers should make sure this list is not used after being passed as an
argument to this function.

This function only sets the @t{use-list} ``slot'' in @var{package}.  It
does not update the @t{used-by-list} ``slots'' of the packages in
@var{new-value}, nor does it check for any conflicts resulting from the
changed use relations.  This function is called by
@genref{use-packages,@middlepackage{}} and
@genref{unuse-package,@middlepackage{}}.
@end deffn

@defgena{used-by-list,@lowpackage{}} client package

Return a list of package objects that contains the packages which are
used by @var{package} @emph{in the package system specified by
@var{client}}.

The returned list is not freshly allocated.

TODO: why "list" in this name but not the other list-typed accessors?

@lowimplements{package-used-by-list,f,package-used-by-list}
@end deffn

@defgena{(setf used-by-list),@lowpackage{}} new-value client package

Set the used-by list of @var{package} to @var{new-value} @emph{in the
package system specified by @var{client}} and return @var{new-value}.

@var{package} must be a package object.  @var{new-value} is a list of
package objects that become the new list of packages used by
@var{package}.  This function does not copy the @var{new-value} list, so
callers should make sure this list is not used after being passed as an
argument to this function.

This function only sets the @t{used-by-list} ``slot'' in @var{package}.
It does not update the @t{use-list} ``slots'' of the packages in
@var{new-value}, nor does it check for any conflicts resulting from the
changed use relations.  This function is called by
@genref{use-packages,@middlepackage{}} and
@genref{unuse-package,@middlepackage{}}.
@end deffn

@defgena{documentation,@lowpackage{}} client package

Return the documentation of @var{package} @emph{in the package system
specified by @var{client}}.

The returned documentation is either a string or @code{nil}.

The function can be used to implement the behavior of calls
@code{(@speclink{f,documentation} @var{package} t)}
where @var{package} is a package object with the following properties

@enumerate
@item

@code{(@genref{packagep,@lowpackage{}} @var{client} @var{package}) @result{} @emph{true}}

@item

@code{(typep @var{package} '@lowpackage{}:package) @result{} @emph{true}}
@end enumerate
@end deffn

@defgena{(setf documentation),@lowpackage{}} new-value client package

Set the document of @var{package} to @var{new-value} @emph{in the
package system specified by @var{client}} and return @var{new-value}.

@var{package} is a package object. @var{new-value} is a string or @code{nil}.

The function can be used to implement the behavior of calls
@code{(setf (@speclink{f,documentation} @var{package} t) @var{new-value})}
where @var{package} is a package object with the following properties

@enumerate
@item

@code{(@genref{packagep,@lowpackage{}} @var{client} @var{package})
@result{} @emph{true}}

@item

@code{(typep @var{package} '@lowpackage{}:package) @result{} @emph{true}}
@end enumerate
@end deffn

@defgena{make-package-object,@lowpackage{}} client name

Create and return a new package object named @var{name} that is suitable
for the package system specified by @var{client}.

@var{name} is a string.

In contrast to the behavior of @speclink{f,make-package}, the returned
package contains no nicknames, no relations to other packages, no
symbols and it is not registered under the name @var{name} in the
package system.  Those aspects have to be set up by separate calls to
other low-level functions.  Furthermore, whether @var{name} already
names a different package is not considered.
@end deffn

@node Low Package-symbol Relation Functions
@section Low Package-symbol Relation Functions

The functions in this protocol are primitive operations for manipulating
the relation between packages and symbols.  The functions are called by
functions in the @ref{Middle Package-symbol Relation Functions} protocol
but there is no one-to-one correspondence.

@cindex symbol entry
@cindex export status
@cindex shadow status
This protocol uses the concept of a @term{symbol entry} which is a
triple consisting of a @speclink{t,symbol} object, an
@term{export status} and a @term{shadow status}.  The information in
such a triple is associated with a symbol name within a package to
indicate the status of the symbol in that package in terms of the symbol
being present in the package, the symbol being external and the symbol
being shadowing.  Concretely, the @termref{export status} is either
@code{:internal} or @code{:external} while the @termref{shadow status} is
either @code{t} or @code{nil}.

@defgena{map-symbol-entries,@lowpackage{}} client function package @
  @optional{} status

Call @var{function} for each @termref{symbol entry} in @var{package}
@emph{in the package system specified by @var{client}} and return
@code{nil}.

@var{function} is a function the lambda list of which has to be
compatible with @code{(symbol export-status shadow-status)} where
@var{symbol} is the symbol object for which the @termref{symbol entry}
is reported, @var{export-status} indicates the @termref{export status}
of @var{symbol} and @var{shadow-status} indicates the @termref{shadow
status} of @var{symbol}.  Any values returned by @var{function} are
ignored.

As is probably obvious, this function does not consider inherited
symbols in any way.
@end deffn

@defgena{symbol-entries,@lowpackage{}} client package &optional status

@quotation warning
Do we keep this function or remove it?
@end quotation

Return as a list of triples the @termrefl{symbol entry, symbol entries}
of @var{package} @emph{in the package system specified by @var{client}}.

The elements of the returned list of the form @code{(symbol
export-status shadow-status)}.
@end deffn

@defgena{symbol-entry,@lowpackage{}} client name package

Look up the @termref{symbol entry} for @var{name} in @var{package}
@emph{in the package system specified by @var{client}} and return three
values:

@enumerate

@item
The symbol object if such an entry exists in @var{package} or @code{nil}
otherwise.

@item
@cindex export status
The @termref{export status} of the symbol in @var{package} if an entry
exists or @code{nil} otherwise.  The export status is either
@code{:internal} or @code{:external}.

@item
@cindex shadow status
A Boolean which indicates the @termref{shadow status} of the symbol in
@var{package}.  @code{t} indicates that the symbol named @var{name} is
shadowing in @var{package}.  @code{nil} indicates that the symbol is not
shadowing, either because it is not interned in package or because it is
interned but not shadowing.

@end enumerate

The most general way to check whether @var{name} names a symbol that is
interned in @var{package} is to check whether the second return value is
@code{nil}.  This is because the first return value can be @code{nil} if
the package system in question uses ordinary @commonlisp{} symbols as
its symbol objects and because the third return value can be @code{nil}
whether the entry exists or not.

As is probably obvious, this function does not consider inherited
symbols in any way.
@end deffn

@defgena{set-symbol-entry,@lowpackage{}} symbol export-status shadow-status @
  client name package

@cindex symbol entry
Create or change the @termref{symbol entry} for @var{name} in
@var{package} @emph{in the package system specified by @var{client}}.

@cindex export status
@cindex shadow status
@var{symbol} is the symbol of the new or changed symbol entry.
@var{export-status} is the @termref{export status} of the new or changed
symbol entry.  @var{export-status} has to be either @code{:internal} or
@code{:external}.  @var{shadow-status} is the @termref{shadow status} of
the new or changed symbol entry.  @var{shadow-status} has to be either
@code{nil} or @code{t}.

This function does not adjust the home package of @var{symbol} nor
consider a potentially existing symbol entry for @var{name}.  This
function also does not check for or handle any conflicts that are caused
by the changes of the export status and shadow status of @var{name} in
@var{package}.

@quotation Note
There is a @code{setf} expander for @code{symbol-entry} which expands to
@code{set-symbol-entry} so that clients can use the syntax
@code{(setf (symbol-entry @var{client} @var{name} @var{package})
            (values @var{symbol} @var{export-status} @var{shadow-status}))}
to call @code{set-symbol-entry}.
@end quotation
@end deffn

@node Low Environment Functions
@section Low Environment Functions

The functions in this protocol are primitive operations for manipulating
the package environment.  The functions are called by functions in the
@ref{Middle Environment Functions} protocol but there is no one-to-one
correspondence.

@defgena{packages,@lowpackage{}} client

Return a list of all defined packages @emph{in the package system
specified by @var{client}}.
@end deffn

@defgena{find-package,@lowpackage{}} client name

Return the package object that has @var{name} as its name or one of its
nicknames @emph{in the package system specified by @var{client}}.
Return @code{nil} if no package has @var{name} as its name or as one of
its nicknames.

@var{name} is a string.
@end deffn

@defgena{(setf find-package),@lowpackage{}} new-value client name

Set the package associated with @var{name} to @var{new-value} @emph{in
the package system specified by @var{client}} and return
@var{new-value}.  If @var{new-value} is @code{nil}, arrange for
@var{name} to be associated with no package.

@var{new-value} is either @code{nil} or a package object.  @var{name} is
a string.

In accordance with the role of the low module, this function does not
check or adjust the name and nicknames of @var{new-value} if
@var{new-value} is a package object.  Neither does this function check
whether @var{name} is associated with a package at the time of the call.
@end deffn
